th a vision of her life in  mind and it somehow felt like she had  lost herself along the way that she  really knew how she was back then but  that had gotten married and had kids  nothing to argue with but wasn't exactly  where she had anticipating being when  she was that age but the more we  excavated her life and the original  Times article that I wrote about her was  called speak memory and this is really  what the process of writing this book  was was just peeling back the layers of  time and revealing her life from the 30s  Florence turned to me and she said you  know you've brought back my life and I  told her will you  back mine too I mean I think this is  speaks to why all of us read and just  our curiosity in life and why we want to  expand our horizons is to learn about  other people and who they are in the way  I was able to when I met Florence bring  back that 14 year old girl to the 9 year  old woman and let the two meet and she's  really provided me with an incredible  perspective on life she's really the  enduring woman of the diary in that she  said go for it she said that's what she  thought her younger self would have said  I don't know many nine year olds who  would speak so matter-of-factly about  sex about their love affairs and just  about who they set out to become in who  they ended up becoming with such honesty  so we read these books we become so  attached to the stories of Florence and  the women in the beauty school in Kabul  and part of the reason why that happens  is because the writing is so clear and  it's so it's a beautifully done that  their stories just come to life and so  I'm curious to know if about your  process of writing nonfiction genre that  is amorphous it's hard to pin down how  do you think about your books as memoir  versus journalism and were there any  particular challenges you dealt with in  the writing process I never I'm a  hairdresser I never said I know and I  never set out to write a book I was  trying to make a living and Holland  Michigan being a hairdresser and I would  I would leave my my clients who had  horrid roots by the time I came back and  I'd be gone for you know first time one  month the second time two months I  stretched it to three months they really  hated me and I needed to keep my  customers because I had you know bills  to pay so I started writing these really  lengthy emails every day I would at the  end of the day I would write down  everything  like just trigger points like what  happened and then I'd sit down when we  had electricity and I would literally  just make these massive emails and I  would send them out and I didn't want I  really wanted to bring my customers and  my friends into my world so that they  understood that like I really needed  them to be my customers when I came back  so I could pay my bills and that was  really kind of how it started but I  never I never saved any of these I just  sent them off and you know went on with  my day and I came back maybe it was  about a year two years into this whole  Afghan process and my one of my very  good friends who is in the book we call  it a 2 meter woman she was 66 foot  something and she was painting the salon  for me and but she she had taken all my  emails and she I don't spellcheck  anything and I did I literally said  there's never a capital letter there's  never periods it's just one giant sand  and I would just send it hood I would  just be typing away and not paying  attention to anything and my English  teacher would it laughs still that there  I have a book but that is why we have  editors but of so they she seek out my  my emails she gotten a law she's spell  checked them  she except a little pictures she put  them in a folder in book form she goes  Debbie here's your book don't stop  writing I'm like I'm not a writer she  goes you're a storyteller  do not stop telling your stories because  you bring us into a world that we don't  know so at that point um that's when  someone introduced me to blogging so  then I began to blog because you know my  emails are just so large and that way I  could add pictures and that's what I  started doing and I started realizing  when I read through that that a book she  gave me I'm like oh I forgot about that  oh I forgot about that and I realized  how many things I had actually forgotten  and so at that point I decided you know  what it's not about them anymore because  now I had actually picked up to move  Cabo's I didn't need to keep my  customers but I needed to not forget and  that's what I was worried about that I  would forget and so that's how that's  how it happened and nonfiction is is  interesting because you forget you it's  really funny because you meet people my  son just got married and you know so  what does he give the in-laws is the  book first so now you know and I keep  going I quit smoking really I quit  smoking and you know it's like you  forget you tell really at the time  you're like into the moment and you're  telling personal things and you forget  that the world knows like things that  you think huh if I thought about that I  might not have said it but you're just  all of a sudden your life is just being  put on paper and it feels okay and then  you realize that you know millions of  people are like reading about your sex  life or you know I mean my mom didn't  know I smoke I don't smoke anymore by  the way I did quit but it's like when  all of a sudden that first page came out  and I looked at the book and I read it  and I thought oh my god my mother's  going to know I smoked and so it is a  very unusual we changed the names of the  girls we've had some issues with the  identity of the women the one thing was  very important an issue that I do like  to just bring to in 2005 women in  Afghanistan could speak their mind they  could work as journalists they could  sing they could do the things that we  thought that we were going there to do  it's 2008 they're killing the  journalists they're stabbing them at  their door there's a thing like American  Idol this thing called Star Search  Afghan star the third runner-up was a  woman which was fabulous she is in  hiding now  so things have gone backwards to an  extensive point  to a point that it's really really scary  to be a woman in Afghanistan now and  where we were had made such amazing  progress in 2005 where the girls are so  excited to tell their story and so  excited they wanted to use a real name  now there now it's like you know people  are trying to punish them for telling  their story and it's very frightening  but it's a way that the land is going  now and you know I think a lot of people  don't realize that how bad it's gotten  from 2006 to 2008 um of course when I  found the diary I already thought it was  a work of art this was the book of this  young woman and my intention was to  return it to Florence and I was just  honored to be brought into her world  into a very different city a city that  she really had ownership over that she  would ride the double-decker bus and  stare into the 5th Avenue apartment  buildings like they were a little  theatrical performance put on just for  her theatre every night when it was  affordable and just this very rich world  when I met Florence she as I told you  said that she was a writer which I knew  from reading the Diaries pages and she  has a real flair for the dramatic and  she knew that I wrote for the New York  Times and she thought isn't this just an  incredible story and of course I thought  it was and so this grew into an article  and my editor at Harpers stumbled upon  the article she lives in the  neighborhood visited the building and  called me up and she said I want to  write read more and of course I had  research piled up to here from going in  excavating Florence's College Archives  in these lines of women with Marcel's  hair and matching v-necks provided by  the photographer names like pearls  Beatrice's Ethel's Gertrude's  Florence's  and I just thought what happened to you  all all these very Stern determined  faces the daughters of immigrants and I  wanted to know what had happened to them  later in life and so the book really  grew out of this diary  it's an amalgam of interviews a memoir  of me finding the diary finding Florence  Florence's world in a nine year old  woman reflecting on the course of her  life and it was really time travel back  to the 30s and Florence and I both  reread the diary and the book is  interspersed with her diary entries so  that each reader really feels like  they're discovering this journal in this  life and the challenge with Florence was  only she turned to me and she said but  I'm not a celebrity I'm not a public  figure and that was really the challenge  to overcome but the one that I thought  was so important which was just the  significance of all of our lives and  that it really didn't matter that she  wasn't a celebrity and wasn't famous she  sailed to Europe in 1936 and traveled  from London to Paris to Rome where she  embarked on a love affair with an  Italian count named Filippo Kenna let  ego dent a duster Rollo who composed  love verses to her which were later  published it was an adventure but also a  very serious and important reflection on  the stories that all of us have to tell  and we always say you know well everyone  has a novel in them well here was a life  which would have otherwise been lost but  was brought to life in Florence now says  she's one of the luckiest women in the  world and is really having this  Renaissance later in life and just being  discovered for being her very true in  authentic self  and now question some of the audience  don't be shy could you please if you  could step up to the microphone in the  center thank you I wanted to ask Deborah  Rodriguez how would you explain the  changes happening in Afghanistan from  2005 to 2008 well outside of the fact  that I'm a hairdresser not like a  political analyst I can kind of just  tell you what I saw there is a there  seems to be a lot of money and raping a  conflict area basically there is I think  I read an email from my girlfriend who's  still there and I the one of the big  problems is embassies are not there  they're reinventing the wheel every day  they're not working as a team with their  projects USAID is is horrible for this  organizations like NGOs nongovernmental  agencies they're not coordinating so  it's like how many schools can you build  Casillas seems like like our military  loves to build schools you can get  funding to build schools  there's no teachers you can build a  hospital hello  there's no doctors so this is what's  happening is there's this an enormous  amount of waste with the money my  girlfriend works with refugees right and  she's she literally wrote an email and  you could see her weeping in it she  works with refugees where they said you  can't live here anymore you got to go  over here and they put them in this  barren field right and so a USAID was  funding a woman's Jirga which is a  woman's meeting for seven hundred  thousand dollars for two days two days  she goes I have people that haven't  eaten for two days okay so now we've got  a few angry afghans  and rightfully so because they see very  very well  some people are making $1,500 a day  that's a lot of money when that's their  income for a year and they're living  very very high I mean you know when  you're there you have a cook you have a  cleaner you have all these things but  the thing is is the money isn't going to  the right places so now you've got  poppies what else are they going to grow  give an alternative there is no  alternative at this point there's  poppies there's mafia there's criminal  activity  you got the insurgency of the Taliban  you have got just people who are tired  of watching the foreigners live high on  the hog and the Afghans are starving  did you realize the winter was so harsh  at one point there was over a thousand  people just froze to death their their  their sheep their goats their everything  we're freezing they're starving there's  no food they can't get wheat Pakistan  has cut off the borders and is only  allowing a portion of wheat to come in  they're robbing the wheat markets  they're hungry they're angry they see  six years of liberation only coming to  the fact there's more suicide bombings  every day than ever  there's roadside bombs suicide bombs  they're bombing there they're inserting  bombs into donkeys we didn't have this  before they didn't have this before  they're not making any money the men are  sitting and they're not able to feed  their family so what did happen I don't  know something didn't go right how I  don't know what the answer is either  because it's so far the other way it's  so bad now that really honest I I pray  for the country every day I am very very  worried and so are the Afghans and so  most of the foreigners that are working  there  gotten bad patient system in place where  the children going to yes they are going  to school but the thing is is you  realize there's no teachers the teachers  are getting paid $40 a month they can't  afford to teach they could work as a  cleaning lady somewhere for a hundred to  one hundred and fifty dollars a month  but the teachers aren't educated they  are not school to teach you know what I  mean they grab all these girls and they  put them to school but there's no proper  teachers because there's no system for  that yet you know the government  corruption it's an enormous amount of  corruption from the very top to the very  bottom i I know of a situation where a  school was so-called funded right the  the person who was funding the school  took the money and when the the person  came to see his school that he built  they brought him to pay paid another  they paid to borrow another school oh  this is your school and these are all  your students and it wasn't he just  pocketed the money I mean it's just  corruption there's so much corruption  but we have not we have not liberated  Afghanistan  that's very clear yes thank you any  other questions please put it down low  just for Debbie are you still doing here  in Holland I know I actually I moved to  California and the weather's really nice  and I'm there now I basically been doing  my book tour and I don't have a license  in California so they won't what I do  here on the side but don't tell anybody  and you can never get away from doing  here but I'm actually working on another  book right now so is it a sequel er it's  about Afghanistan there's a lot of  trafficking of women through the guise  of marriage  kind of on long that line and Lily are  you also working another book I am  working on another book one of the  things that was really meaningful to me  about writing the red leather diary was  Florence's daughter's reaction to the  book both are in their 60s she also has  granddaughters and great-grandchildren  and her daughters not only you know did  they find out things about their mother  that they had never known her affairs as  both men and women and just how  passionate she was as a young writer  they said that they had just learned so  much about her from reading the book  just seeing her not is their mother but  is this person with this whole other  life before they were part of the  picture and this really is made me think  recently and just look at my mother in a  different way and that's the question  I'm going to be probing really how do we  see our mothers and do we really know  them do we know them how we know our  friends and I'm going to be telling her  story which starts in World War two and  ends up in Newport Rhode Island where  her father was the physician to JFK at  the summer White House and it's this  young woman's coming of age her years at  Pembroke and going to San Francisco for  the summer of love wonderful and a quick  follow-up question about Florence Wilson  you mentioned that she would she said  that she didn't know how she had ended  up living in ordinary life after keeping  this diary that was full of you know  affairs and a glamour how did that  happen what what happened to - this is a  woman who is a passionate writer writer  I mean isn't yeah Farris and you know  glamour but I mean she was really a rare  combination of both beauty and intellect  she remembered from her literary salon  bending down to light the fireplace in  her parents apartment as her  guess Delmore Schwartz and John Berryman  were debating the poetics of Aristotle  and st. Thomas Aquinas and as she bent  down to unpin her long blonde hair let  it cascade down and so she really was  aware of both being as sharp as a whip  intellectually but also her powers of  seduction but how did this happen well  Florence's parents both Russian Jewish  immigrants really wanted her to sew up  her life as fast as possible this summer  there were trips to the Catskills and  they wanted her to get married and marry  a nice doctor and you know just make  herself secure that was enough for them  but for her she wanted this whole other  dimension out of life which was one of  art and beauty and in the 1940s after  she got married she did have a stint  writing feminist Tinch articles for big  magazines at the time that published all  of the great writers Upton Sinclair's  and Claire Sinclair Lewis like Good  Housekeeping Ladies Home Journal  Cosmopolitan articles such as how to  behave in public without an escort or  how to quarrel with your husband but  Florence in a way gave in to the  conventions of her time and her parents  but you know there was always that  internal searching voice within her that  knew that she wanted to do more and I  think that's really the beauty of the  story of the red leather diary which is  that her words and her just her passion  and ambition has come full circle to her  and this incredibly serendipitous way  through another young writer late in her  life and it is really just rewritten the  end of her life she turned to me and she  said my life is a tragedy she's very  dramatic I said no it wasn't Florence  but she said but now it's a romantic  comedy  and she recently got a laptop and is  thinking about writing again and keeping  a diary even though she she said she's  actually just happy breathing and  feeling more alive than she has in years  and she's about to celebrate her 93rd  birthday in August and suddenly she's  become this Authority people are asking  her opinion on things I mean this is  like Florence jokes it's like sexy  Tuesdays with Maurey but it's real and I  think that's an important thing to  remember about the red leather diaries  this store is larger than the book and  it's about these two people who  connected and everybody who connects  with its message which is about the  significance of all of our lives these  are both extraordinary portraits and  extraordinary women so thank you both  for being here and sharing these stories  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  right  I always looking for like history  biography and public affairs you're  watching book TV on c-span 2 this  weekend were live from the printers Row  Book Fair in Chicago  coming up next Marc Bouwer line author  of the dumbest generation and Rob Walker  on buying in and then a panel featuring  David Mendel author of Obama and Blake  Dvorak of RealClearPolitics calm
 

